{
  "question_num": 2,
  "total_marks": 16,
  "title": "Forces, Upthrust and Elasticity",
  "main_stem": "A hot-air balloon floats just above the ground. The balloon is stationary and is held in place by a vertical rope, as shown in Fig. 2.1. The balloon has a weight $W$ of $3.39 \\times 10^4 \\mathrm{N}$. The tension $T$ in the rope is $4.00 \\times 10^2 \\mathrm{N}$. Upthrust $U$ acts on the balloon. The density of the surrounding air is $1.23 \\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-3}}$.",
  "parts": [
    {
      "label": "(a)(i)",
      "part_stem": null,
      "text": "On Fig. 2.1, draw labelled arrows to show the directions of the three forces acting on the balloon.",
      "marks": 2,
      "answer_prompt": null,
      "unit": null
    },
    {
      "label": "(a)(ii)",
      "part_stem": null,
      "text": "Calculate the volume, to three significant figures, of the balloon.",
      "marks": 3,
      "answer_prompt": null,
      "unit": null
    },
    {
      "label": "(a)(iii)",
      "part_stem": null,
      "text": "The balloon is released from the rope. Calculate the initial acceleration of the balloon.",
      "marks": 3,
      "answer_prompt": null,
      "unit": null
    },
    {
      "label": "(b)",
      "part_stem": null,
      "text": "The balloon is stationary at a height of $500 \\mathrm{m}$ above the ground. A tennis ball is released from rest and falls vertically from the balloon. A passenger in the balloon uses the equation $v^2 = u^2 + 2as$ to calculate that the ball will be travelling at a speed of approximately $100 \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ when it hits the ground. Explain why the actual speed of the ball will be much lower than $100 \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ when it hits the ground.",
      "marks": 3,
      "answer_prompt": null,
      "unit": null
    },
    {
      "label": "(c)(i)",
      "part_stem": "Before the balloon is released, the rope holding the balloon has a strain of $2.4 \\times 10^{-5}$. The rope has an unstretched length of $2.5 \\mathrm{m}$. The rope obeys Hooke's law.",
      "text": "Show that the extension of the rope is $6.0 \\times 10^{-5} \\mathrm{m}$.",
      "marks": 1,
      "answer_prompt": null,
      "unit": null
    },
    {
      "label": "(c)(ii)",
      "part_stem": null,
      "text": "Calculate the elastic potential energy $E_{\\mathrm{P}}$ of the rope.",
      "marks": 2,
      "answer_prompt": null,
      "unit": null
    },
    {
      "label": "(c)(iii)",
      "part_stem": null,
      "text": "The rope holding the balloon is replaced with a new one of the same original length and cross-sectional area. The tension is unchanged and the new rope also obeys Hooke's law. The new rope is made from a material of a lower Young modulus. State and explain the effect of the lower Young modulus on the elastic potential energy of the rope.",
      "marks": 2,
      "answer_prompt": null,
      "unit": null
    }
  ],
  "markscheme": [
    {
      "label": "(a)(i)",
      "marks": 2,
      "marking_points": [
        {
          "tag": "B2",
          "text": "arrow upwards ($\\uparrow$) and labelled upthrust / $U$; arrow downwards ($\\downarrow$) and labelled weight / $W$ / $mg$; arrow downwards ($\\downarrow$) and labelled tension / $T$; 1 mark: One or two correctly labelled arrows; 2 marks: Three correctly labelled arrows"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "label": "(a)(ii)",
      "marks": 3,
      "marking_points": [
        {
          "tag": "C1",
          "text": "$U = T + W$ or upthrust = tension + weight"
        },
        {
          "tag": "C1",
          "text": "$\\rho Vg = T + W$; $V = [(4.00 \\times 10^2) + (3.39 \\times 10^4)] / (1.23 \\times 9.81)$"
        },
        {
          "tag": "A1",
          "text": "$V = 2.84 \\times 10^3 \\mathrm{m^3}$"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "label": "(a)(iii)",
      "marks": 3,
      "marking_points": [
        {
          "tag": "C1",
          "text": "$m = W / g$ or $a = F / m$"
        },
        {
          "tag": "C1",
          "text": "$a = (4.00 \\times 10^2) / [(3.39 \\times 10^4) / 9.81]$"
        },
        {
          "tag": "A1",
          "text": "$a = 0.12 \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}$"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "label": "(b)",
      "marks": 3,
      "marking_points": [
        {
          "tag": "B1",
          "text": "there is air resistance (which increases with speed)"
        },
        {
          "tag": "B1",
          "text": "(average) resultant force is less (than weight)"
        },
        {
          "tag": "B1",
          "text": "(average) acceleration is less (than $g$ / 9.81, so speed is less than $100 \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "label": "(c)(i)",
      "marks": 1,
      "marking_points": [
        {
          "tag": "A1",
          "text": "(extension =) $2.5 \\times 2.4 \\times 10^{-5} = 6.0 \\times 10^{-5} (\\mathrm{m})$"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "label": "(c)(ii)",
      "marks": 2,
      "marking_points": [
        {
          "tag": "C1",
          "text": "$E_{(P)} = 1/2 Fx$ or $E_{(P)} = 1/2 kx^2$ and $F = kx$"
        },
        {
          "tag": "A1",
          "text": "$E_{(P)} = 1/2 \\times 4.00 \\times 10^2 \\times 6.0 \\times 10^{-5}$ or $E_{(P)} = 1/2 \\times 6.7 \\times 10^6 \\times (6.0 \\times 10^{-5})^2$; $E_{(P)} = 0.012 \\mathrm{J}$"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "label": "(c)(iii)",
      "marks": 2,
      "marking_points": [
        {
          "tag": "M1",
          "text": "longer extension or smaller spring constant"
        },
        {
          "tag": "A1",
          "text": "elastic potential energy is greater"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
